  {
    "episode_id": 97119,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/7/97119.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Yes, yes. It's really a tragic, tragic, horrible story coming out of Newark. And I just don't think the nation has focused enough on it. A 14-year-old kid is charged with shooting and killing a Newark police officer. Now, as I said in my column, just stop and think about that set of facts right there. 14 years old, what is he, eighth grade, maybe freshman in high school? If he's in school, we don't know a lot about the kid yet. But what is he doing with a gun and what happened where he took a shot at a police officer and actually killed him? And that's what I think our country is just not focusing enough on. It's become extremely dangerous in some parts of cities. And police officers are right at the front of it. I come to this. My first book was about a police shooting, and I was highly critical of the fact that the police officer shot a young 15-year-old African-American kid. But I understand, I think, some of the pressures that police are all under. I've taken the deadly force test. I've gone to the police academy and studied the problems of deadly force and what cops are up against. And it takes a very unusual person to be a police officer. But I think the way people are armed now in",
